{"cuisine/barbadian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/barbadian-cuisine","n":"Barbadian cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Barbados","intro":[["Barbadian cuisine, also called Bajan cuisine, is a mixture of African, Portuguese, Indian, Irish, Creole, Indigenous and British background. A typical meal consists of a main dish of meat or fish, normally marinated with a mixture of herbs and spices, hot side dishes, and one or more salads.  The meal is usually served with one or more sauces."],["The national dish of Barbados is cou-cou and fried flying fish with spicy gravy. Another traditional meal is pudding and souse, a dish of pickled pork with spiced sweet potatoes. A wide variety of seafood and meats are also available."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"barbadian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbadian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Barbados Bu'n-Bu'n: My Culinary Adventure","ref":"cookbook/barbados-bu-n-bu-n-my-culinary-adventure","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Rosemary Parkinson","note":"Two-volume survey of Barbadian food, cooks, and recipes.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9789768225849","suggested":true}]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"cuisine/hunan-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/hunan-cuisine","n":"Hunan cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in China","intro":[["Hunan cuisine, also known as Xiang cuisine, consists of the cuisines of the Xiang River region, Dongting Lake and western Hunan Province in China. It is one of the Eight Great Traditions of Chinese cuisine and is well known for its hot and spicy flavours, fresh aroma and deep colours. Despite this, only about 20% of the cuisine uses capsicum to produce a strong spicy taste. Common cooking techniques include stewing, frying, pot-roasting, braising and smoking. Due to the high agricultural output of the region, many different ingredients are used in Hunan dishes."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"hunan-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hunan_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Revolutionary Chinese Cookbook: Recipes from Hunan Province","ref":"cookbook/revolutionary-chinese-cookbook-recipes-from-hunan-province","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Fuchsia Dunlop, 2007","note":"Dunlop's study of Hunanese home and restaurant cooking.","url":"https://wwnorton.com/books/9780393062229","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":2,"links":[["Changsha stinky tofu","dish/changsha-stinky-tofu"],["West Lake Restaurant","dish/west-lake-restaurant"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/ba/Fried_Pork_with_Pepper_20210630.jpg/500px-Fried_Pork_with_Pepper_20210630.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"Huangdan2060","lic":"CC BY 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fried_Pork_with_Pepper_20210630.jpg"}},"cuisine/solomon-islands-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/solomon-islands-cuisine","n":"Solomon Islands cuisine","c":"#55b8ad","crumb":[["Oceania","cuisines#oceania"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Solomon Is.","intro":[["Cuisine of the Solomon Islands has developed over 5,000 years of inhabitation and external influences. From the Spanish, the islands received cattle; from the Asians and Indians, spices, exotic vegetables and fruit."],["The islands were later colonized by the English, who left their own culinary mark. The main occupations of the locals are fishing and agriculture, so fish, coconuts, cassava, sweet potatoes and a variety of fruits and vegetables figure into the local cuisine."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"solomon-islands-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solomon_Islands_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Eat Pacific: The Pacific Island Food Revolution Cookbook","ref":"cookbook/eat-pacific-the-pacific-island-food-revolution-cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Robert Oliver (ed.), 2024","note":"Pacific Island Food Revolution cookbook with a Solomon Islands section.","url":"https://masseypress.ac.nz/books/eat-pacific","suggested":true}]}],"cont":"Oceanic cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/0/05/Bowl_of_poi.jpg/500px-Bowl_of_poi.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Bshams","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bowl_of_poi.jpg"}},"dish/crocche":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/crocche","n":"Crocchè","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["A croquette is a deep-fried roll originating in French cuisine and most extended in Spanish cuisine consisting of a thick binder combined with a filling, which is then breaded. It is served as a side dish, a snack, or fast food worldwide."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-crocche"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crocch%C3%A8"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Neapolitan cuisine","cuisine/neapolitan-cuisine"],["Sicilian cuisine","cuisine/sicilian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":7,"links":[["Béchamel sauce","ingredient/bechamel-sauce"],["Brown sauce","ingredient/brown-sauce"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Ham","ingredient/ham"],["Mashed potato","ingredient/mashed-potato"],["Shellfish","ingredient/shellfish"]]},{"h":"More from Neapolitan cuisine","links":[["Casatiello","dish/casatiello"],["Pastiera","dish/pastiera"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/f2/Crocch%C3%A9_di_patate_%28Naples%29.jpg/500px-Crocch%C3%A9_di_patate_%28Naples%29.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Catia Giaccherini","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crocch%C3%A9_di_patate_(Naples).jpg"}},"ingredient/demi-glace":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/demi-glace","n":"Demi-glace","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Demi-glace is a rich brown sauce in French cuisine used by itself or as a base for other sauces.  The term comes from the French word glace, which, when used in reference to a sauce, means \"icing\" or \"glaze\".  It is traditionally made by combining one part espagnole sauce and one part brown stock. The sauce is then reduced by half, strained of any leftover impurities, and finished with a sherry wine."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-demi-glace"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demi-glace"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Yoshoku","cuisine/yoshoku",[["Hayashi rice","dish/hayashi-rice"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Mushroom","ingredient/mushroom"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Red wine","ingredient/red-wine"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Tomato sauce","ingredient/tomato-sauce"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/9d/C%C3%B4te_de_Porc_Fermi%C3%A8re.jpg/500px-C%C3%B4te_de_Porc_Fermi%C3%A8re.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Edward Russell","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:C%C3%B4te_de_Porc_Fermi%C3%A8re.jpg"}},"ingredient/quail":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/quail","n":"Quail","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Quail is a collective name for several genera of mid-sized birds generally placed in the order Galliformes. The collective noun for a group of quail is a flock, covey, or bevy."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-quail"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quail"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Czech cuisine","cuisine/czech-cuisine",[["Kulajda","dish/kulajda"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Dill","ingredient/dill"],["Mushroom","ingredient/mushroom"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Sour cream","ingredient/sour-cream"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/7/73/Brown_Quail.jpg/500px-Brown_Quail.jpg","w":500,"h":414,"by":"Duncan Wright","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brown_Quail.jpg"}},"dish/lanzhou-beef-noodles":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/lanzhou-beef-noodles","n":"Lanzhou beef noodles","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Chinese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Lanzhou beef noodles is a type of beef noodle soup from Lanzhou, China. This dish has an extensive history and is intimately associated with Hui Muslim cuisine. The dish has since become widespread throughout China, with 50,000 noodle shops in the country serving the dish as of 2015."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-lanzhou-beef-noodles"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lanzhou_beef_noodles"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":4,"links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Beef broth","ingredient/beef-broth"],["Chinese noodles","ingredient/chinese-noodles"],["Lamian","ingredient/lamian"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"The Woks of Life","ref":"site/thewoksoflife-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"thewoksoflife.com","note":"Chinese and Chines