ojo (sauce)","dish/mojo-sauce"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Coriander","ingredient/coriander"],["Vinegar","ingredient/vinegar"],["Bulgur","ingredient/bulgur"],["Lens","ingredient/lens"],["Lentil","ingredient/lentil"],["Lentils","ingredient/lentils"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"]]}]},"dish/ohaw":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/ohaw","n":"Ohaw","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Ainu cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Ohaw or rur is a savory soup of Ainu cuisine, a dish of the Ainu people of northern Japan, flavored with fish or animal bones. Kelp is also used to add flavor to the stock. Unlike the majority of the traditional Japanese soups, the Ainu do not use miso or soy sauce in their soups. The solid ingredients such as meat, fish, vegetables and/or wild edible plants are added to the stock."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-ohaw"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ohaw"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Ainu cuisine","cuisine/ainu-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Fish","ingredient/fish"],["Kelp","ingredient/kelp"]]},{"h":"More from Ainu cuisine","links":[["Munini-imo","dish/munini-imo"],["Rui-be","dish/rui-be"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/a0/Ainu_ohaw.jpg/500px-Ainu_ohaw.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"jetalone","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ainu_ohaw.jpg"}},"dish/omelette":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/omelette","n":"Omelette","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["An omelette is a dish made from beaten eggs (usually chicken eggs) fried with butter or oil.  Unlike scrambled eggs, they are cooked undisturbed so that they set into a continuous disk. In Europe, omelettes 